    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that's a good start. I think the students might struggle with the \"pressure difference\" part vs the \"action/reaction\" part though. Could you maybe use a quick analogy or a little scenario to help them visualize how that air is actually interacting with the wing surface? Something that makes it feel more intuitive rather than just abstract physics.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No problem! Think about a garden hose. When you spray water straight up, you can see a narrow stream above the hole. This happens because our fast-moving spray adds energy and speeds up the water, creating a vacuum underneath which then draws the water upwards (similar to air over a wing causing lower pressure). Now, if you pointed the hose upwards into the air (like a wing in the air), the water air would hit the sides of the hose and quickly exit. Newton's third law comes into play—the water shoots out fast, and that reaction forces the hose upwards (think of the wing moving up due to the high-speed air pushing down on it). That's the simple physics of lift: fast-moving air creates low pressure, and the reaction between the fast-moving air and the slower air below creates an upward force on the wing. This combination of forces keeps an airplane flying. Can we give the students a handout with a diagram to illustrate this better?",
      "source": "target",
